<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dangun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dangun Wanggeom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dangun is the legendary founder of Gojoseon, the first Korean kingdom. The thirteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> describes him as the son of a heavenly prince and a bear transformed into a woman, and dates his founding of the state to 2333 BCE. He is honoured as the mythical ancestor of the Korean people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> marks National Foundation Day on 3 October in his memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dangun.jpg" alt="Dangun" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chae Yong-sin (1850-1941) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Dangun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the legend, Tan'gun's grandfather was Hwanin, the Lord of Heaven. Hwanin's son Hwanung yearned to live on the earth among the valleys and mountains, and with 3,000 followers he descended onto Taebaeksan, now believed to be Paektu Mountain, where he founded Sinsi, the City of God. With his ministers of clouds, rain and wind he instituted laws and moral codes and taught humans the arts, medicine and agriculture. A tiger and a bear prayed to become human. Hwanung gave them twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> of garlic and a bundle of mugwort and told them to eat only this and stay out of the sunlight for 100 days. The tiger left after about twenty days, but the bear persevered and became a woman, Ungnyeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Ungnyeo made offerings to Hwanung but had no husband, and she prayed beneath a divine birch tree for a child. Hwanung took her as his wife, and she bore a son, Tan'gun Wang'gŏm. He ascended the throne, built the walled city of Asadal near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, though its location is disputed, and called his kingdom Joseon, now called Gojoseon to distinguish it from the later Joseon kingdom. The bear and the tiger are said to represent two tribes that sought the favour of the heavenly prince.</p><p>His reign is usually reckoned from 2333 BCE, following the Tongguk t'onggam of 1485, and sources place it in the reign of the Chinese emperor Yao, though they differ on the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The earliest recorded version of the legend is in the 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a>, which cites the lost Gogi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Book of Wei, though the surviving Book of Wei holds nothing about Tan'gun. Similar versions appear in the Jewang Un-gi of Yi Seunghyu and in early Joseon records.</p><p>Tan'gun drew attention in the late Goryeo dynasty, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> fought the Mongol Yuan dynasty, and the Joseon dynasty built shrines to him. At the end of the 19th century he became a figure of resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, and he was central to nationalism and the independence movement under Japanese rule. Until 1961 the official South Korean era, the Dangi, counted years from 2333 BCE. National Foundation Day on 3 October was begun as a religious anniversary by Daejongism, which worships him, as do the followers of Cheondoism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tan'gun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Korean tradition credits its founding to the mythical <b>Dangun</b> in 2333 BCE, but the historical state appears in Chinese sources from about the 7th century BCE as a power of the Liao river basin and northwestern Korea, linked to the Liaoning bronze dagger culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/gojoseon">Gojoseon</a></li></ul></section>
